  "worked_solution": "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=\\frac{15}{2}$ and $c=\\frac{39}{2}.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{39}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\,521}{4}-\\frac{225}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
